## Summary

With eager SSD offload enabled, `GroupOffloadingKeysByBucket` defers the **tail partial bucket** of each drain cycle to `ungrouped_offloading_objects_` so it can be written as part of a full bucket next cycle. But the deferral hand-off loses the key's `OffloadTaskItem`:

1. the deferred keys are **NACKed to the master in the same cycle** (they appear in no bucket, so the end-of-`OffloadObjects` sweep reports them with the `data_size = -1` sentinel), which erases their `offloading_tasks` entry and releases the source-replica refcount;
2. next cycle, the backend prepends the deferred keys into the first bucket — but `FileStorage::OffloadObjects` filters every bucket key through `task_by_storage_key`, which is **rebuilt from that cycle's drain only**. The deferred keys have no task, so they are **silently discarded**: never queried, never written, never notified, and (being inside `all_bucket_keys`) not even NACKed again.

Net effect: **every key that lands in the deferred tail is dropped from the offload pipeline with no log and no error.** The object keeps no SSD replica, its refcount pin is gone, and it is eventually memory-evicted — a cache entry lost while the SSD tier has free space. Since deferral fires whenever a drain's key count isn't a multiple of the effective bucket capacity, this is a steady-state leak, not an edge case.

## Code walk (`main` @ `fc04cb1`)

Drain is destructive — the master forgets the queue when the worker heartbeats (`master_service.cpp:4934`):

```cpp
local_disk_segment_it->second->offloading_objects.clear();
```

Deferral carries key+size only, not the task (`storage_backend.cpp:2313`):

```cpp
ungrouped_offloading_objects.emplace(bucket_object.first, bucket_object.second);
VLOG(1) << "Add offloading objects to ungrouped pool. ...";
return {};
```

The same-cycle sweep NACKs them (`file_storage.cpp:642`) — the comment lumps *deferred* keys in with *deliberately skipped* keys (size-limit / already-on-disk), for which NACK mop-up is correct:

```cpp
// Keys skipped by GroupOffloadingKeysByBucket don't appear in any bucket,
// so they never reach BatchOffload or complete_handler.
for (const auto& [storage_key, task] : task_by_storage_key) {
    if (all_bucket_keys.find(storage_key) == all_bucket_keys.end()) {
        failed_tasks.push_back(task);
    }
}
```

And next cycle the task-less deferred keys are dropped without a trace (`file_storage.cpp:516`):

```cpp
for (const auto& storage_key : keys) {
    const auto it = task_by_storage_key.find(storage_key);
    if (it != task_by_storage_key.end()) {
        storage_keys_by_tenant[it->second.tenant_id].push_back(storage_key);
    }
    // else: silently dropped — no store, no notify, no NACK, no log
}
```

## Production evidence

Observed on a 3-node shared pool (eager offload, ~5-object/~236 MB effective buckets, drain cycle ~10 s):

- **~12% of pool exits have no matching disk eviction** (cumulative last-replica exits minus disk-tier evictions) — i.e. that share of objects dies RAM-only while the SSD tier is under its eviction watermark with free space.
- Worker-side `VLOG(1)` attribution over 60 min on one node: `already exists` skip **0**, `size exceeds` skip **0**, D2H/store failures **0**, task-not-found **0** — but `Add offloading objects to ungrouped pool` **209** (≈ one deferral per drain cycle, matching `... have been processed` at 210).
- Master-side NACK count ≈ the RAM-only exit gap (~113–127% cumulatively; NACKs lead because the NACK happens at PUT-side drain, the exit at later eviction).
- Keys sampled from the NACK path and probed against the master's index minutes later: **19/21 absent** (contemporaneous control keys via the identical query: 21/21 present).

## Relationship to #2632 / #2658 — this is #2658's silent regression

Before #2658, these same deferred keys leaked *loudly*: their drained-but-unresolved tasks held a stale refcount pin until the 600 s TTL and produced the `Offloading task expired` warning storm. #2658's NACK sweep now cleans them up promptly — but since the deferral was never distinguished from a deliberate skip, the fix converted a visible leak into a silent one. We measured the same ~9–13% loss share both before and after deploying #2658; only the disposal path changed. (Verified with #2658 included: the NACK sentinel machinery works as designed — the problem is that deferred keys should never have been NACKed.)

Also related but distinct: #2827 / PR #2967 fix a different caller bug in the same function (whole-cycle abort on `OBJECT_ALREADY_EXISTS` skipping the NACK flush). #2967 does not touch the deferral hand-off; the two fixes compose.

Same silent-disposal family as #2997.

## Suggested fix

Either:

- **(A) Carry the task across the deferral.** Have `AllocateOffloadingBuckets` report which keys it deferred (out-param or getter); `FileStorage` keeps a small `deferred_task_by_storage_key_` map, excludes those keys from the NACK sweep, and merges the carried tasks into the next cycle's `task_by_storage_key` so the deferred keys complete normally. Keeps the refcount pin alive across the (one-cycle, ~10 s) deferral, which also guarantees the memory replica is still there to read. Bounded by one partial bucket.
- **(B) Flush the tail partial bucket instead of deferring.** Simpler; removes `ungrouped_offloading_objects_` entirely at a small write-efficiency cost (one sub-full bucket per drain cycle).

Happy to open a PR for (A) if that direction is acceptable.

Environment: observed on `v0.3.12-pre1` + #2658; code path verified byte-identical on `main` @ `fc04cb1`.

ModelWarmup is available as an experimental feature in AIBrix v0.8.0. We would like more users and cluster operators to try it and share what works, what is unclear, and what fails in real environments.

ModelWarmup prepares selected Kubernetes nodes before workload placement. It can:

- preload container images;
- run finite custom preparation actions, including model downloads and node checks;
- run once against a resolved set of nodes;
- continuously cover nodes as they join, leave, or are replaced.

ModelWarmup creates a node-pinned Job for each selected node. It prepares caches only. It does not start an inference server or gate workload placement.

The feature is experimental in v0.8.0, so its API and behavior may change based on feedback.

References:

- [AIBrix v0.8.0 release](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/releases/tag/v0.8.0)
- [ModelWarmup documentation](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/blob/v0.8.0/docs/source/features/model-serving/model-warmup.rst)
- [ModelWarmup samples](https://github.com/vllm-project/aibrix/tree/v0.8.0/samples/modelwarmup)

## Start with a small smoke test

After installing AIBrix v0.8.0, authorize a test namespace and node in the same resource pool:

```bash
kubectl label namespace default resource-pool.aibrix.ai/name=latency-pool
kubectl label node <node> \
  resource-pool.aibrix.ai/name=latency-pool \
  model.aibrix.ai/warmup-enabled=true
```

Apply the lightweight image-only sample:

```bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vllm-project/aibrix/v0.8.0/samples/modelwarmup/modelwarmup.yaml
kubectl get modelwarmup runtime-image-warmup -w
```

The sample pulls `busybox:1.36` on each authorized target node and exits. A successful run reaches the `Succeeded` phase.

## What we would like tested

We are interested in feedback from different Kubernetes distributions, container runtimes, registries, storage configurations, and GPU environments.

Useful test areas include:

- image preloading with public and private registries;
- custom model downloads and node-local cache permissions;
- node and GPU prechecks;
- the combined vLLM and SGLang samples;
- `Once` behavior with multiple nodes and partial failures;
- `Continuous` behavior when nodes join, leave, are relabeled, or are replaced;
- retries, timeouts, and controller recovery;
- taints, Pod Security admission, quotas, and restricted namespaces;
- status, events, diagnostics, and documentation clarity.

Successful test reports are useful too. Please include the environment and sample you tried so we can understand where ModelWarmup is already working.

## Reporting a problem

For a bug, open a separate issue and link back to this one. Please include:

- AIBrix version or commit;
- Kubernetes version and container runtime;
- ModelWarmup manifest with credentials removed;
- expected and actual behavior;
- `kubectl describe modelwarmup <name>`;
- generated Job and Pod status or events;
- relevant controller logs.

## Contributing

Pull requests are welcome for bug fixes, tests, documentation, diagnostics, and sample improvements. Please link the PR to the reported problem and describe how you tested the change.

For API changes or larger behavior changes, start with an issue or discussion before opening a large PR. This gives users and maintainers time to agree on the requirement while the feature is still experimental.

