Following up on my January comment. #32081 helped (~68s to ~60s in @junuxyz's test above) but it still pays the strided gather on the CPU, just over smaller slices. I took the direction @robertgshaw2-redhat suggested to its conclusion in #47580: keep the copy source as the contiguous on-disk slab and do the transpose as a strided write into the parameter on the GPU side. No copy of the loaded weight is kept in GPU memory at any point.

On 1x A100-80G (warm cache): per-copy time on Scout-size fp8 expert slabs goes 62.8ms to 4.0ms (15.8x), and end to end on a 24-layer Scout FP8 truncation (full model doesn't fit one 80G card) load time goes 18.8s to 12.5s. This applies to any fused-MoE checkpoint that arrives transposed, not just Llama-4/ModelOpt. If anyone can run the full model on cc >= 8.9 hardware I'd appreciate the datapoint.

@pratheeknathani #46766 takes the same approach as #32081, so the ~60s numbers above should carry over to it.

@holtwood Thanks, good catch. Confirmed on `1ca53a8`: the deadline check runs before `query_read_status()`, so an already-finished transfer is still reported as failed when the poll lands after the deadline.

This definitely looks worth covering with a regression test. Querying the transfer status before applying the timeout seems like a natural direction, though whether a `DONE` result first observed after the deadline should still count as success is part of the broader timeout semantics here.

If you'd like to contribute the test for this case, that would be very welcome. Otherwise I'll make sure to credit you for identifying it in the PR I'm planning for this.

Opened #5482 as a draft for the late-poll case. It queries the transfer status before applying the load deadline, so an already-terminal read is reported with its actual result.

The in-flight timeout/ownership case (A / B / C above) is intentionally unchanged and still open.

@holtwood I included an adapter-level regression test for the case you found. I left the NIXL handle-cleanup-specific test out, so if you'd still like to add that, feel free to post the test here or open a PR targeting my branch and I'll include it with credit.

Confirmed on main: `PutEnd` returns success even when `PushOffloadingQueue` fails (no local disk segment → `SEGMENT_NOT_FOUND`; full queue → `KEYS_ULTRA_LIMIT`), with nothing logged or counted — the object silently loses its only chance to reach the SSD tier.

I have a patch for the observability part ready: an `offload_enqueue_failed` counter in `MasterMetricManager` plus a sampled warning (`LOG_EVERY_N(WARNING, 100)`) in the `PutEnd` else branch, mirroring what the eviction-path callers already do, with gtest coverage for both failure modes (all master suites pass, 179/179).

@ccgibson you mentioned you'd be happy to PR this as well — since I already have it working, I'll go ahead and open the PR in a bit; very happy to take your review on it.

@albanD My current draft impl, also sets `__name__` attributes recursively as proposed in:
- https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/104247

Certainly name construction can be improved somehow (https://github.com/vadimkantorov/torchlisttensors)

```python
# References:
# - https://discuss.python.org/t/list-all-objects-gc-tracked-or-not-of-a-given-type-for-debugging-introspection-and-memory-profiling/98194/5
# - https://www.mail-archive.com/numpy-discussion@scipy.org/msg46120.html
# - https://github.com/szagoruyko/pytorchviz/blob/0adcd83af8aa7ab36d6afd139cabbd9df598edb7/torchviz/dot.py#L146

import gc
import sys

import torch

def torch_list_tensors(*roots):
    SAVED_PREFIX = "_saved_"
    seen = set()
    nodes = {}

    def found_tensor_hook(tensor, name):
        if id(tensor) not in nodes:
            nodes[id(tensor)] = name

    def get_var_name(var, name = '', from_var = None):
        if not name:
            #name = param_map[id(var)] if id(var) in param_map else ''
            if hasattr(var, '__name__'):
                name = var.__name__
            else:
                name = '__' + str(id(var)) + '__'
        return 'shape=({})\tname={}'.format(','.join(map(str, var.shape)), name)

    def add_nodes(fn, from_var = None):
        assert not torch.is_tensor(fn)
        if fn in seen:
            return
        seen.add(fn)

        fmtvarname = lambda varname, saved = False: varname + '_{}_{}'.format(type(fn).__name__, from_var) + ('_SAVED' * saved)

        if True:
            for attr in dir(fn):
                if not attr.startswith(SAVED_PREFIX):
                    continue
                val = getattr(fn, attr)
                seen.add(val)
                attr = attr[len(SAVED_PREFIX):]
                if torch.is_tensor(val):
                    varname = get_var_name(val, attr, from_var = id(fn) )
                    found_tensor_hook(val, fmtvarname(varname, saved = True))
                if isinstance(val, tuple):
                    for i, t in enumerate(val):
                        if torch.is_tensor(t):
                            varname = get_var_name(t, attr + '[{}]'.format(i), from_var = id(fn))
                            found_tensor_hook(t, fmtvarname(varname, saved = True))

        if hasattr(fn, 'variable'):
            var = fn.variable
            seen.add(var)
            varname = get_var_name(var, from_var = id(fn))
            found_tensor_hook(var, fmtvarname(varname))

        if hasattr(fn, 'next_functions'):
            for u in fn.next_functions:
                if u[0] is not None:
                    add_nodes(u[0], from_var = id(fn))

        if hasattr(fn, 'saved_tensors'):
            for t in fn.saved_tensors:
                seen.add(t)
                varname = get_var_name(t, from_var = id(fn))
                found_tensor_hook(t, fmtvarname(varname))

    def add_base_tensor(var):
        if var in seen:
            return
        seen.add(var)
        found_tensor_hook(var, get_var_name(var))
        if var.grad_fn:
            add_nodes(var.grad_fn, from_var = id(var))
        if var._is_view():
            add_base_tensor(var._base, from_var = id(var))

    seeds = roots[:]
    for objs in [gc.get_objects(), sys.getobjects() if hasattr(sys, 'getobjects') else []]:
        for obj in objs:
            try:
                if torch.is_tensor(obj):
                    tensor = obj
                elif hasattr(obj, 'data') and torch.is_tensor(obj.data):
                    tensor = obj.data
                else:
                    continue
                if hasattr(tensor, '__name__'):
                    found_tensor_hook(tensor, get_var_name(tensor))
